{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791184393,"nonce":"0D7sFHKa+lZJJU8+A9/HErYc4mo=","title":"Gltf: an option to cap texture size on load","message":"`Gltf.load(path, {maxTextureSize: 1024})` would downscale any image larger than the cap, keeping its aspect ratio, before it becomes a texture. Blinc's loader has the same option (blinc_game_kit/src/gltf/mod.rs, `LoadOptions::max_texture_size`).\n\n**Why:** BC compression (a4d46b6) already brought the helmet's textures from 112MB to about 16MB, so this is no longer needed for memory. It is still useful for:\n- models with many 4K textures, where even compressed textures add up (the strangler rig in Blinc's assets has 29);\n- faster loads, since resampling and compressing scale with pixel count;\n- small viewports, where full-resolution mips are never sampled.\n\n**How:**\n- Add an optional `options` argument to `Gltf.load` and `Gltf.parse`.\n- Resample once to the capped size natively (`blinc_bitmap_resample`), then decode a new bitmap from that, or add a Bitmap method that downscales in place, so the full-size pixels are freed before upload.\n- MeshTextures then makes its mips and BC levels from the smaller image.\n\n**Test:** load the helmet with the cap at 512 and check the texture's size and that it still renders.","files":null}]}